  "msg.about": "About Arcana",
  "msg.aboutTagline": "Classic spirit, modern skin.",
  "msg.aboutSupported": "Supported formats:",
  "msg.aboutFormats": "zip, 7z, rar, tar, gz, bz2, xz, zst, cab, arj, lzh, lzma, br, lz4, snappy",
  "msg.aboutPoweredBy": "Built with Avalonia & CommunityToolkit.Mvvm",
  "msg.aboutEngines": "Compression engines: SharpCompress, Hawkynt FileFormats, ZstdNet, K4os.LZ4, Snappy, Konscious.Argon2",
  "msg.aboutIcons": "Icons: Arcana, Numix, Tango, Papirus, La Capitaine",
  "msg.aboutThanks": "Thank you for using Arcana!",
  "msg.aboutStory": "Arcana is built on a simple belief: your files should never be held hostage by a format. Written in pure C#, it speaks 17 archive and compression formats natively, plus a pure-C# fallback engine that recognizes roughly 240 more. No native binaries, no external tools, no dark magic under the hood.\n\nSecurity is first-class: AES-256-GCM for encryption and Argon2id for key derivation. Splitting, joining, hashing with verification, conversion and benchmarks all ship built-in, and a full-featured CLI mirrors the GUI. More than 190 tests guard every engine.\n\nThe name is no accident. An arcanum is a hidden secret, a recipe for the initiated \u2014 and every archive is a locked grimoire waiting for its reader.\n\nArcana is crafted by Marcelo Frau, senior solutions architect at adidas, a Brazilian living in Europe who has been writing software since 2003. By day, distributed systems; by night, retrogaming and emulation \u2014 he writes trespordez.com.br as \u201cThe Archivist\u201d, restores and mods classic consoles, and keeps the emulation world alive with projects like xb-homebrew-vault and OpenBurningSuite.\n\nFun fact: Marcelo wrote hjsplit2 in Go \u2014 the seed of Arcana\u2019s splitting tools. And as an Arctic Code Vault contributor, his code is literally archived in a time capsule for future generations. Some arcanums are meant to be preserved.",
  "msg.aboutDetail": "Arcana \u2014 archive manager\n{0}\n\nClassic spirit, modern skin.\n\nSupported formats: {1}",
